3. Discussion

The team reviewed the existing PIC16F72 Microcontroller Simulator, which was initially developed using Java.

During the discussion, the team identified difficulties in demonstrating POSIX-based inter-process communication (IPC) using Java alone.

The team proposed introducing Python alongside Java to simplify the implementation of POSIX IPC mechanisms and process communication.

The following points were discussed:

Java would continue handling the main simulator logic and graphical user interface.

Python would be introduced to handle POSIX-related IPC operations.

Communication between Java and Python would be established to exchange instructions and execution results.

Existing simulator functionality and user interface should remain unchanged.

The updated implementation should maintain the same core outputs while demonstrating operating system concepts.

4. Decisions Made

After discussion, the team collectively agreed to:

Change the implementation from Java-only to a combination of Java and Python.

Retain Java for the simulator's core functionality and user interface.

Use Python to implement POSIX IPC mechanisms.

Integrate both languages to demonstrate inter-process communication.

Preserve existing functionality and expected simulator outputs.

Test the updated implementation to ensure reliable communication between processes.

